I'll continue the investigation by reading the key theme and config files to pin down the exact cause.Let me gather the missing ground-truth: daisyUI's injected base styles, the current built CSS state, and the git history of the theme CSS.The daisyUI base layer is the smoking gun candidate. Let me inspect the full daisyUI package and the compiled CSS artifact that ships.Now I can see the compiled output. Critical finding: daisyUI defines a global `.menu` component class. Let me verify how VitePress styles the navbar's right-side menu element, which shares that exact class name.Let me locate the actual VitePress style files.Let me read the VitePress styles index to find the exact component file names.Found it. Let me confirm whether daisyUI components are actually used anywhere in the docs (to decide between a targeted override vs. removing the plugin), and inspect the home page that uses Tailwind utilities.Let me do a final verification that no daisyUI components are used anywhere and check for custom Vue components.I now have everything needed. Let me apply the fix to the theme CSS.Let me verify the written file.## 1. Analysis & Pre-Thinking

**Core Requirements** — The docs navbar is broken: the right-side navigation menu appears as a *floating, detached box*. The goal is to restore the standard VitePress navbar so the right menu sits inline (horizontal) inside the top bar.

**Root cause (found)** — It is a **class-name collision between daisyUI and the VitePress default theme**, not the Tailwind utilities.

1. VitePress renders the navbar's right-side menu with an extra class:
   `node_modules/vitepress/dist/client/theme-default/components/VPNavBar.vue`
   ```vue
   <VPNavBarMenu class="menu" />
   ```
   …and its own style only declares `display`:
   ```css
   /* VPNavBarMenu.vue <style scoped> */
   .VPNavBarMenu { display: none; }
   @media (min-width: 768px) { .VPNavBarMenu { display: flex; } }
   ```
   So the element is `<nav class="VPNavBarMenu menu">`.

2. `.vitepress/theme/custom.css` loads `@plugin "daisyui";`, and daisyUI ships a **global `.menu` component**:
   ```css
   .menu {
     display: flex;
     width: fit-content;
     flex-direction: column;   /* ← stacks the nav links vertically */
     flex-wrap: wrap;
     padding: 0.5rem;
     font-size: 0.875rem;
   }
   ```

3. Because Tailwind/daisyUI output is inside `@layer utilities` while VitePress styles are **unlayered**, VitePress wins only for the properties it actually declares (`display`). Every property VitePress does **not** declare (`flex-direction`, `width`, `padding`, …) is taken from daisyUI → the right menu becomes a padded, fit-content, **vertical** box → "floating".

4. The same collision hits the navbar separator: VitePress renders `<div class="divider">` (`VPNavBar.vue`) and daisyUI also defines `.divider` with `margin: 1rem 0` plus extra `::before`/`::after` lines.

**Strategic Approach** — daisyUI contributes **nothing** used by the docs (the content only uses plain Tailwind utilities, e.g. `flex`, `grid-cols-3`, `rounded-md`, `bg-gradient-to-br from-sky-800 …` in `index.md`). Removing the plugin eliminates the collisions at the source, instead of patching each colliding class.

**Dependencies & Risks** — Low risk. Tailwind tokens + utilities are kept intact; only the unused daisyUI plugin is dropped. No `.md` file uses any daisyUI component class.

**Target Files** — `.vitepress/theme/custom.css` (modify only).

---

## 2. Minimum Changes Approach

**File: `.vitepress/theme/custom.css`**
- Remove the single line `@plugin "daisyui";`.
- Extend the header comment documenting *why* daisyUI must not be re-added (collisions with `.VPNavBarMenu.menu` and `.divider`).
- Keep everything else identical: layer declaration, `tailwindcss/theme.css` + `tailwindcss/utilities.css` imports, brand variables and `.feature-icon` helper.

No other file needs to change. (`public/custom.css` is a stale, unreferenced build artifact — the site loads the theme CSS via `.vitepress/theme/index.ts`, so it can stay as-is.)

---

